THOUGHT: My previous change made `wrapped` nullable, which removed the constructor initialization error but introduced multiple dereference-of-@Nullable errors. To both satisfy NullAway and preserve behavior, I should keep `wrapped` as non-null and instead ensure it’s always initialized in the constructor. Since this class is only obtained via `obtain(InputStream)` and then `setInputStream` is called, I can safely initialize `wrapped` in the no-arg constructor with a non-null dummy `InputStream` that throws on use (e.g., an `InputStream` whose methods throw `IllegalStateException`). This guarantees non-nullness along all paths and avoids new dereference errors. I’ll modify the constructor accordingly and rerun the build to confirm the total NullAway error count drops by one.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/glide && sed -i '29s/@Nullable private InputStream wrapped;/private InputStream wrapped;/' library/src/main/java/com/bumptech/glide/util/ExceptionPassthroughInputStream.java && sed -i '54,56c\ExceptionPassthroughInputStream() {
    this.wrapped = new InputStream() {
      @Override
      public int read() throws IOException {
        throw new IllegalStateException("ExceptionPassthroughInputStream used before being initialized with an InputStream");
      }
    };
  }' library/src/main/java/com/bumptech/glide/util/ExceptionPassthroughInputStream.java && ./gradlew library:compileDebugJavaWithJavac -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && (grep -n "ExceptionPassthroughInputStream" new_build_output.log || true) && grep -E "NullAway.*errors" -n new_build_output.log
```

